<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>In our case, we were pro=
cessing 50MB CSV files of transaction data</strong>, extracting small ident=
ifiers (like transaction IDs or account numbers), but the entire original C=
SV was being retained in memory because our extracted strings were just sli=
ces pointing back to the original buffer.</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>The symptoms were subtle=
</strong> - memory usage would steadily climb during bulk imports, and we'd=
 occasionally get out-of-memory errors on lower-end devices. The debugging =
was tricky because heap snapshots showed strings we thought we'd discarded.=
</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Interviewer</strong>: Pe=
rfect example. You've touched on the core mechanism. Let me dig deeper into=
 your understanding of the V8 internals. Can you explain what exactly happe=
ns in V8's memory when you create a sliced string? And more importantly, ho=
w would you differentiate between a sliced string and a regular string when=
 debugging?</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>Candidate</strong>: Grea=
t question! Let me break down the V8 internals:</p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>V8 String Representation=
:</strong></p>
<p class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words">In V8, strings can have differen=
t internal representations:</p>
<ul class=3D"[&amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [&amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:p=
b-1 list-disc space-y-1.5 pl-7">
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>SeqString</strong>: Seq=
uential strings where characters are stored contiguously</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>ConsString</strong>: Co=
ncatenated strings (tree structure for efficiency)</li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>SlicedString</strong>: =
Our problem child - contains a pointer to parent string + offset + length</=
li>
<li class=3D"whitespace-normal break-words"><strong>ExternalString</strong>=
: Points to external memory (like native code)</li>
</ul>
